Description: pvr: calibrated timestamps on CLOCK_MONOTONIC_RAW
 The firmware's job timestamps are in ns of CLOCK_MONOTONIC_RAW, so the device
 time domain is that clock. Set vk_device.get_timestamp to read it and enable
 VK_KHR_calibrated_timestamps and VK_EXT_calibrated_timestamps with the common
 entry points, only with a kernel that reports job timestamps. zink then reads
 GL_TIMESTAMP without a round trip to the GPU (zink_get_timestamp).
 .
 vk_common_GetCalibratedTimestampsKHR() adds device_time_domain_period to the
 maximum deviation it returns. The job timestamps are nanoseconds, but the
 firmware takes them from the GPU CR timer, and one tick of it is
 ROGUE_CRTIME_TICK_IN_CYCLES = 256 core clock cycles, 640 ns at the lowest
 operating point of the GPU, 400 MHz. The device sets device_time_domain_period
 to the tick length at that clock, rounded up to whole nanoseconds, instead of
 the ceil(timestampPeriod) of 1 ns that vk_device_init() sets. The kernel
 interface does not report the clock range, so it is a constant here and the
 value is a bound that holds at every higher clock, where a tick is shorter.
 timestampPeriod stays 1 ns per unit because the reported values are
 nanoseconds.
 .
 Only for this downstream stack; not intended for upstream submission.
